      "content": "The spider/goblin analogy can be a bit...goblin-ish. Let's dive into the math behind the interest calculation.\n\nThe formula for calculating the interest on a loan is:\n\nInterest = Principal x Rate x Time\n\nWhere:\n\n* Principal is the current outstanding balance\n* Rate is the annual interest rate (as a decimal)\n* Time is the number of years the loan has been outstanding\n\nHowever, since you're making monthly payments, we need to adjust the formula to account for the monthly payments. The formula becomes:\n\nInterest = (Principal x Rate x (Number of Months Outstanding) / 12) x (1 + Rate/12)\n\nThis formula calculates the interest accrued on the loan up to the current month.\n\nNow, let's break it down further:\n\n* The `(Number of Months Outstanding) / 12` part is used to convert the annual interest rate to a monthly rate.\n* The `(1 + Rate/12)` part is used to calculate the interest accrued on the loan over the past month.\n\nFor example, let's say you have a loan with a principal balance of $10,000, an annual interest rate of 6%, and you make a monthly payment of $200.\n\nIn the first month, the interest",
